 days  and then they're also these  re-challenged specimens  in contrast the human um decedents will  have  entered the hospital and proceeded  in many cases fairly rapidly through  disease in some cases in as little as 48  hours  in other cases over the course of  several  weeks at which point those who passed  away and  volunteered to have  have their tissues examined would then  be subjected to the analysis that i'm  going to show you so we have these two  different diseases  also separated by two different species  so one of the biggest surprises for us  when we did this it's only two months  ago now  was that actually we can get around 7000  proteins quantified  uh on an nhp specimen or a human  specimen about  the same and that would contrast for  these types of profiling techniques deep  shotgun proteomics of around 10 to 11  000 proteins on the best  possible frozen specimen so this is  actually a surprisingly  deep look at what is after all a very  small  specimen only five microns thick and  that can be  uh fixed and therefore preserved in the  case of the oncology studies that's  hopeful be  helpful excuse me because we can go back  in time in the case of  the covid studies it'll also allows us  to inactivate virus which means that  all of these studies can be done under  low biocontainment  so what actually do you see under these  circumstances so  let me show you what i'm going to call  single sample  gene set enrichment but it's not  actually genes it's proteins so we  use all of the tools that have been  developed for rna  transcript analysis but here we apply  them to proteins and not surprisingly we  see extremely strong  interferon alpha and gamma responses so  that's the classical viral  response what's nice isn't exactly the  same specimen only five microns away  we can then do the high resolution  imaging that i  showed you a little bit about earlier  and we can do this in all sorts of  different channels and we can go in and  ask okay are  which cells in fact are interferon  activated the answer is it's both immune  epithelial and endothelial cells what  are the  different types of cells that are  recruited and we can enumerate  macrophages t  cells b cells etc  we find under these circumstances uh  that for  proteins like mx1 which is an interferon  induced  antiviral protein that across the  specimens we have there's a surprisingly  good correlation between  the levels that we measure by mass  spectrometry  and the levels we bother by imaging on  on match  specimens so that gives one some  confidence that these two very different  modalities  are returning results that can be  integrated  with each other as i mentioned briefly  earlier actually it's also possible to  look at phosphorylation which seems to  us  particularly surprising we're not  currently doing that  in the autopsy specimens because of the  uneven period  between death and the beginning of  fixation but in the case of these  nhp specimens that was very well  controlled and so we can now see these  time courses  the samples are going from control at  the bottom and pink  up to the re-challenge at the top so  imagine a resolved infection  and for ifit three a classic interferon  response factor you can see that it  becomes quite strongly phosphorylated  at day two which is also the peak of the  interferon response  and so this and i'll come back to how  these pieces fit together again  but this allows us to look at the signal  transduction events  um that take place in these specimens  now one of the surprising things when we  took this initial set of samples and  we've done it a few times and seen  the same basic result was we saw a  fundamental difference between  uninfected specimens and infected  specimens from  the right to the left but within any  given  time point and even within a single lung  we saw a big difference you can see 2 a  and b  on the top and 2 c and d on the bottom  and we were really  sort of concerned about that why why was  there a difference between  uh the specimen uh in in these analyses  which seemed otherwise to be quite  reproducible  and the answer of course lay in these  region to region  differences that you see so here's a  single  lung specimen from from one of the  non-human primates and within one of  those lungs this will be taken at day  two  and we're looking at here a macrophage  marker in red in the background this is  very  low magnification so you're looking at  almost three centimeters of tissue  uh here across at this scale what we see  is there are regions that are relatively  normal  r4 at the top we see regions that are  positive  for virus and we can just detect that  directly based on spike and capsid  protein staining  they've recruited a few macrophages but  then finally the regions r3 is an  example that are called consolidated and  those can  be seen even when you do cat scanning  and those are the regions of the lung in  which the alveoli have  filled up either with fluid or with  other  immune cell types and you've lost the  respiratory function  and these regions are actually  remarkably different from each other  even though they've been recovered from  a single animal  and you can see now on the right the  uninfected we see this picovirus in day  two those are the orange and black dots  with a lot of difference from one  specimen to the next  and then that goes way down by day four  the animals are already successfully  clearing  the virus positive virus positivity  presumably through this interferon type  response and so we can then take what  would otherwise be sort of  a confounder in the analysis and turn it  into a critical variable so we can look  directly at the relationship  even in a single specimen between virus  load  and the load of macrophages and other  consolidants  relative to the biology and i think  that's a simple example  of the value of getting these spatially  resolved pictures because  uh in almost all of the things that we  would look at  either in this infectious disease or in  cancer really the region to region  differences are nearly as great as they  are  from one patient to the next  and here's just one other view where  i've shown some additional markers and  you can see how  different that is the consolidated  region if you look in the upper panel  there you can see a whole bunch of  what we call reactive or activated  inflammatory macrophages cd16 positive  and you can see those are absent from  that relatively normal region  the consolidated region at r3 remember  is virus negative and the presumption is  those cells have already cleared the  virus and that it was an earlier stage  of the infection  so there are many similarities between  animals and human responses i've just  pulled out a couple of these again  interferon genes should in this context  be seen as interferon induced proteins  um you can see that big peak at day two  in the high virus sample  from the non-human primates that's the  red stripe going across the  upper cluster diagram and you can see  something approximately similar in  humans where  there seems to be a lot more interferon  induction in patient  [Music]  one and two than there is in patient  three and we'll return to that in a  moment  and if you look at some of these  pathways as a sort of classic reactant  pathway and analysis  you can see that interferon signaling is  up we're seeing a lot of remodeling  of the extracellular matrix there are  other chemokines and cytokines that are  activated  and these are sort of features of the  disease that have also been observed in  the blood and are things that we  expected to see but one of the  biggest surprises from this preliminary  analysis was that even if interferon  responses alpha and gamma here are  strong in humans  if we now look at viral burden and  compare that to inflammation  you'll see that they are uncorrelated or  even in this small  cohort they're actually anti-correlated  so that's very different than what's  seen in animals and in animals is what  we would actually expect for infectious  diseases of that type the greater the  viral burden the greater the induction  of the interferon response  here we see something quite different  which is the greater the viral burden  potentially the less the inflammatory  response  but it's fundamentally uncoordinated and  that from work from 10 over and others  is becoming  clear as a characteristic of covid19 and  terminal patients and humans that is to  say that  the virus and the response to the virus  become  incorrectly coordinated and you no  longer see that natural  course of evolution and that's the  one of the single largest differences we  see between humans and these naturally  resolving animal infections  let me show you two other differences um  the first is these cytokine storms  what's observed in  humans is there are many cytokines that  are upregulated  here i show you a few of them and it's  an interesting feature  of the mass spectrometry that it can  directly measure the levels of soluble  cytokines in the microenvironment  in situ and that this includes uh  classic chemokines and cytokines like  il1 beta for example  as well as a whole series of  interleukins and some are up and some  are down and  they vary from one protein to the next  but decedent number two in our  experiments had a particularly acute  cytokine and sporm involving a wide  variety  of these factors and i've just pulled a  couple of them out below you can see in  the case of  il-1 beta for example this enormous  induction in patient two  um perhaps 10 to 10 to 50 fold  and that's also a sodium with up  regulation of its  one of the components of its receptor  il1  wrap the other thing we can  detect in humans but we see relatively  little and i'll get back to this in a  moment  in uh in in animals is the  end stage of clotting so one of the most  serious complications of covid infection  in humans is  thrombosis and the formation of these  inappropriate clots  which which are thought in some cases to  be the cause of death  and so a factor 13a at the end  takes different residues in fibrin  they're multiple crosslinks  but the one that we find most abundantly  in the mass spectrometer is shown here  between a glutamate and a lysine at  416 and 237 and you can see we can  actually detect very significant  increases both in fibrin itself so in  the protein that actually makes up the  clot  end in the formation of the crosslink so  that's a very nice  end stage of biochemical end stage that  you're seeing in a tissue specimen  because you know that's really  the biology that's driving these  thrombotic processes  so very clear in humans thought to be  relevant we can detect many steps  what do we see in in non-human primates  and this  came as a substantial surprise to us and  that was that the  the actual level of platelet complement  and fibrin activation so those are  different aspects of that  uh thrombotic response if anything  they're actually down regulated  in the non-human primates and you can  see that as we go from sort of the red  stripe and the controls  remember when you scale these things you  see normal here potentially as red  they actually dip down into the blue so  they're down regulated  significantly over dozens of genes and  then they  return when these animals have largely  resolved the infection  and so to understand that actually we  turn once again to the spatial  transcript profiling  and we thought we would compare the  regions i showed you before so what  happens if we look in an area within a  single lung of an infected animal  between virus positive and consolidated  regions  and we compare that to a no virus region  and an uninfected control  and in this case now what we see is the  activation of the platelets fibrinogen  lectins etc so  the difference between humans and  the uh rhesus macaques is  in humans we have delocalized thrombotic  activation we have  clots all the way through the lungs that  eventually propagates into the blood and  becomes  uh life-threatening in the animals in  contrast  this is a highly localized reaction and  as best we can tell it's followed by the  activation  of the enzymes involved in the  dissolution of clots and so you see this  homeostatic regulation and in fact  clotting is a normal part of the  response  to serious viral infection that should  be localized  and then should be shut down so the  striking thing in the human patients is  that  normally homeostatic mechanism has  broken down  and that really explains the difference  we think  between these two different types of  infections  so one of the more interesting aspects  we feel of this type of  multi-um omic analysis involving imaging  transcription and proteomics is you can  now begin to look at many  steps in a cascade not just as gene sets  which i've shown you a little bit as  but also as biological pathways  so in the particular case of cobit 19  we're able to see inflammasome  activation i didn't show you that in  immune cells that would be expected to  lead to production of il1  beta and we can actually detect that  directly  by mass spectrometry we can see the  receptors going up we can see nf kappa b  going up in some cases for some of these  pathways we can see  transcription factors becoming  phosphorylated and then we can see the  sequelae both in terms of  response genes and here's a little  enrichment plot by gene said in richmond  or alternatively in terms of cellular  phenotypes or things like fibrinogen  activation  that i showed you a moment ago now  what's sort of the  sort of the current state of this  analysis and i think  one of the things we've we've come to  see is the immune landscape is really  extremely complicated so here are the  m1-like macrophages as a gene set in  richmond again remember that's protein  space  extremely strongly enriched so that  would be taken as evidence of the  activation  of inflammatory macrophages you can see  down  below in which the different days day  two  and then day two with high virus day  four d14 etc  we're actually seeing macrophage  activation particularly  in the high virus case and then the  right is a sort of medium resolution  view in which  we're seeing a series of immune markers  that would be  uh would allow us to look at t cells b  cells  and some types of macrophages and you  can see in these lungs this  extraordinary recruitment  of different immune cell types leading  eventually to consolidation and  in many cases we don't actually fully  understand  the macrophage types here we don't  understand the marker combinations  that they involve and that's an area of  both now joint molecular  and image based analysis in humans even  more strikingly once again there's the  activation of these macrophages but when  we go from one patient to the next  we actually see dramatic differences  even if macrophages  are present in those patients they  actually have different phenotypes  so just if you look at the left hand  column at the very lowest resolution  you're seeing different resolutions to  the right you can see  h1 at the top row there is red h2 is  green and down below  there is much less of anything and that  represents  two different polarization states of  macrophages in h1n and h2 and then  h3 which remember that's the decedent  with the highest viral load remarkably  enough  has a very impaired macrophage-based  immune response it was also the patient  with a remarkably poor interferon  response  and that was a patient i should say that  actually also had substantial thrombosis  so we can now begin to see the sort of  decoupling of these things that would be  normally part of of the response to a  virus like this  so let me just conclude by saying a  little bit about  what we've started to discover in the  inflammatory and thrombotic features of  covid  i should say cobit 19. excuse me so on  the left  are sort of those micro-environment  features between the blood  the lung parenchyma and different  alveolar cell types and we're able to  look at some of those pathways we've  looked at a lot of gene set enrichment  analysis over the last 20 minutes or so  but in some cases we can trace complete  mechanisms  and on the right we're seeing this  aspect that seems  particularly strong in sars infection  and that's really the thrombotic  component in which you get deposition of  plaques  and i think the evidence we have has  suggests that in animals this occurs as  we would expect it in a localized and  resolving fashion  but in humans with their comorbidities  it becomes runaway  and so better understanding of these  immune environments i think is going to  clearly require  this kind of localized analysis as well  as much better understanding of  individual  cell types involved and that's sort of  where things stand for  the purpose of this and we will be  releasing um the data that i'm showing  you today in the next  month or two because we think this is  the kind of thing that's effectively  distributed to the community  for joint analysis so the last  note to say is there's a lot of work  still to be done here  in making atlas data shareable so the  type of images that i showed you today  are currently shared almost entirely by  people sitting around a microscope this  is actually a contemporary picture on  the left  and uh and and  it's actually a hands-on experience  where you're sort of led through  and the analogy we're using is like a  dos sent shown on the right where a  docent takes you through a museum  and we've actually built a series of  software platforms i don't have time to  show you today but actually allow you to  be led  through a through the atlas data that  i've shown you today in a kind of guided  manner  and that's known as project minerva and  here are some resources you can find i  think you can search for it on our  github  so thank you very much for your  attention um it's really been a pleasure  talking to you today and i'd like to  thank the people who did this work  all of our high dimensional spatially  resolved imaging is done  as a collaboration with sandro santagata  who's a practicing pathologist  we have multiple pathologists helping  with us and i should also call out dan  baruch and amanda martino who's  are responsible for giving us the the  non-human primate specimens and robert  perdera and his colleagues  for the human decedents now expanding to  30. and we've  got a group of software engineers  clinicians and postdoctoral fellows who  contributed to this  and all of whom are continuing to try to  get the steady  data ready for release and then just  thank you for the  this in which we're embedded uh which is  20 different research groups from all  across  massachusetts all working on developing  diagnostics and  modern approaches to therapeutic  discovery thank you very much  thank you so much dr sarger it was a  wonderful presentation  if you'd be so kind we have just a few  questions for you uh and the first one  is what are  some of the challenges and opportunities  in using the spatially resolved data in  analysis of tissue specimens  and what is current state of the art  so i think these are all um newly  developed methods  um so uh the and and as i alluded to in  my talk there is a difference between  what can be done with a frozen specimen  particularly in genomics where  dissociative rna sequencing is very  powerful  and what is possible with these clinical  types of specimens where you have  fixed material and therefore it's a  relatively greater challenge  so i think we have we have really uh  sort of two sets of challenges and let  me give you a quick opportunity the  first set of challenges just be  familiarizing ourselves with with new  methods  um and you can see even from my talk  today with the  proteomics that we can use gene said  enrichment analysis et ceter