ke the early days  of sequencing data  with this strong spatial dimension so i  think it's very exciting  i agree with that for sure uh and  obviously you know the  the work that we're doing with covet is  is brand new and we're inventing new  fields but there's a large body of  evidence that we can draw on already  from the research been done in oncology  so  do you think that the immune  environments that have been established  in the tumor  are similar to what's going on in the  infectious disease or  are the results that you've seen purely  related to the methodologies that you've  used to profile each  yeah so it's a great question you know i  in in a talk like this you know i'm no  infectious disease expert we're  largely in oncology and so we we tend to  link these things  together by the method that we use such  as the opportunity and the challenge  presented by the current pandemic  but actually i think we've become much  more interested in the  in the question as you cast it is what  actually is the difference between  the response say to an acute and a  chronic infection they're different from  each other  um as compared to to what we see in in a  cancer and i can say that  from the current profiling that's coming  up there there are surprising  similarities and differences which is to  say that there are  cell types particularly in the case that  we've looked at in macrophages  that are predominant in the end-stage  cova diseases that we actually  don't see in the acute disease and we we  also don't see in oncology so they're  marker combinations and transcriptional  states that that are probably very very  rare in those other circumstances so i  think actually integrating that  will be interesting and then there are  diseases  like tuberculosis where in the lung you  actually have  these long-term uh sort of individually  evolving  foci that look a bit like a tumor so  they're actually quite different from  each other both with respect to their  microenvironment and their genetics so i  think that's really the the comparison  we'd like to do and i think many people  are becoming very interested in that  yeah i agree there's certainly a lot of  complexity in the immune response and a  lot of  data that's generated as a result of  these multi  profiling mechanisms so can you speak a  little bit about the challenges in  sharing the data and  and making it available and useful for  the community  so i you know i think for many of us  involved in this we're this is an area  of considerable concern  i think that this is you know transcript  data in principle it's  depositable in you know geo in the same  repositories that we have but  that those repositories don't have any  concept of  of space so if we were to deposit you  know 30  gsp data sets or picks or individual  specimens that had a particular  geometry we don't know how we would  deposit that the imaging data  that we have has no repository in the  united states  europeans actually have been well ahead  in developing repositories for some of  these types of data  but we're in the i think worrying and  somewhat peculiar position of having  huge amounts of data coming along all of  which is intended  tended by congress to be shareable  aggressively shareable  for which we have none of the  infrastructure for sharing  um and so i think that's an area that's  going to need a lot of attention  and um and and we don't want to repeat  some of what happened with the big  genomic data sets  which is analysis came way after data  collection  and so we're hoping to get you know the  companies with deep expertise in this  google amazon these companies to  actually engage  in as part of their effort i think  particularly in cobit 19 and helping  make the resources available to get this  data into the hands of people who can  interpret it  i i agree and i think copenhagen  certainly presents a unique opportunity  for us to create these  these shared resources that we need can  you speak a bit about your plans for  creating and releasing the covet 19 data  atlas that you described  yeah so our commitment and that has been  to release it pre-publication  so as soon as the data is sort of  basically analyzed we'll get it out  there and it will  go into three separate locations sadly  one of them is of course the proteomics  data will go into pride where people  hopefully can find it we'll try to build  a website that gives you the index  the spatial transcript profiling will go  into  into the geotype data set but we're  going to have to work out just on an ad  hoc basis how we combine the spatial  information and at the moment there's no  good way  to share the image data outside of this  kind of technology that i briefly  alluded to this sort of  ghost scent based thing minerva but that  that'll actually come out as movies so  um so i think it's it's we're committed  to releasing it it's really the absence  and i think many other  people working in this space it's it's  it's really the need for the  infrastructure  and the standards um is going to have to  have to be developed in real time  wow i agree well thank you so much dr  sterner for  taking time to speak with us today for  sharing with us your exciting  pre-publication data we're looking very  much forward to seeing the publications  come out and seeing how the work  advances  well you should be able to find them on  med archive or bioarchive  as soon as we finished writing and thank  you very much  thank you very much for your time for  inviting me to this exciting event and  for your questions
 

